  "notes": "First Q-simple, geometrically split example of [10] for the census (the gsplit cell is empty). Found by querying the LMFDB genus 2 data (devmirror.lmfdb.xyz, g2c_curves: is_simple_base = true, is_simple_geom = false, torsion_subgroup = [10]; geom_end_alg = M_2(Q), end_alg = CM (LMFDB notation)); smallest conductor among the curves of this group that the census verifier certifies (576; the extended database also has 576.b.1 with the same conductor). LMFDB conductor 576. Checked locally with the census's own pipeline/magma/verify_lib.m (Magma V2.30-1): J(Q)_tors = [10], class gsplit, Q-simplicity prime 19 (chi = T^4 - 22*T^2 + 361), splitness certificate: richelot over Q ((2,2)-isogenous over Q to a Weil restriction of an elliptic curve E over Q(sqrt(2)) (the surface is E x E^sigma)). Submitted by F. Najman; the curve is due to the database cited under Reference. Rigorous conductor: J is (2,2)-isogenous over Q to Res_(K/Q) E with K the quadratic field of discriminant 8, so N(J) = |d_K|^2 * Norm(f_E) = 64 * 9 = 576 (computed with Magma; conductor is an isogeny invariant).",
